course: dictionary
lesson: 15617
title: Tatty: old and worn
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Tatty is an informal word, used most in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. It describes something that is old, worn out and a bit shabby. «Mum put my tatty old coat in the bag for the charity shop.» The word old tells you the coat is not new, and tatty tells you it is worn and not in good shape.
explain: Tatty can also mean something is cheap-looking or of poor quality. «The party decorations were a bit tatty, with paper that was already torn.» Notice the clues: torn paper means the decorations were not well made or were already falling apart.
explain: You can use phrases like «a bit tatty», «tatty old» and «tatty around the edges». Each one tells you the thing is worn, but not completely ruined. «My teddy bear is tatty around the edges, but I love him.» This shows the bear is loved and used a lot, not thrown away.
fact: «Tatty» means old, worn out and a bit shabby.
fact: «Tatty» can also mean cheap-looking or of poor quality.
fact: Tatty is informal, so you would use it in chatty talk and stories, not in a formal letter.
fact: In the USA, people usually say «shabby» or «beat-up» instead of tatty.
fact: Words like «tatty» show a thing has been used a lot, which is why stories often use it for old clothes, books and toys.
exercise: «Ben wore his tatty old trainers every day.» What does tatty mean here? | *Old and worn out | Brand new and shiny | Very expensive | Wet from the rain
exercise: «The tatty red sofa sagged in the middle.» Find and copy one word that means worn out. | red | middle | *tatty | sofa
exercise: Lily says a tatty toy is a toy that is brand new. Is she right? | Yes, tatty means brand new | Yes, tatty means very expensive | *No, tatty means old and worn | No, tatty means very big
test: «My library book is a bit tatty, with torn pages and a cracked spine.» What does a bit tatty suggest? | Brand new and perfect | Written in another language | *Used, worn and not in good condition | Too heavy to carry
test: Dad says, «Your jumper's looking a bit tatty, mate.» Which word from the lesson could replace tatty here? | *Shabby | Enormous | Empty | Glittery
exam: «The old tatty book was still his favourite.» Which word means the same as tatty here? | Expensive | Shiny | *Worn out and shabby | Brand new
